    54	**ABSTRACT**
    55	
    56	
    57	Rust programming language is gaining popularity rapidly in building reliable and secure systems due to its security guarantees and
    58	outstanding performance. To provide extra functionalities, the Rust
    59	compiler introduces Rust unstable features (RUF) to extend compiler
    60	functionality, syntax, and standard library support. However, these
    61	features are unstable and may get removed, introducing compilation
    62	failures to dependent packages. Even worse, their impacts propagate through transitive dependencies, causing large-scale failures
    63	in the whole ecosystem. Although RUF is widely used in Rust, previous research has primarily concentrated on Rust code safety, with
    64	the usage and impacts of RUF from the Rust compiler remaining
    65	unexplored. Therefore, we aim to bridge this gap by systematically
    66	analyzing the RUF usage and impacts in the Rust ecosystem. We
    67	propose novel techniques for extracting RUF precisely, and to assess
    68	its impact on the entire ecosystem quantitatively, we accurately
    69	resolve package dependencies. We have analyzed the whole Rust
    70	ecosystem with 590K package versions and 140M transitive dependencies. Our study shows that the Rust ecosystem uses 1000
    71	different RUF, and at most 44% of package versions are affected by
    72	RUF, causing compiling failures for at most 12% of package versions.
    73	To mitigate wide RUF impacts, we further design and implement a
    74	RUF-compilation-failure recovery tool that can recover up to 90%
    75	of the failure. We believe our techniques, findings, and tools can
    76	help stabilize the Rust compiler, ultimately enhancing the security
    77	and reliability of the Rust ecosystem.
